  "query_preview": "..Visible satellite and radar imagery shows the center \nof the MCV is passing west of Flagstaff, near Williams, and moving \nin a north- northwest direction, producing a quick burst of showers \nand thunderstorms, and then leaving behind a layer of mid- to upper- \nlevel cloud cover. This cloud layer is helping to suppress new \nconvection across Prescott, Sedona, Flagstaff, and surrounding areas \nthis afternoon. However, areas that have remain mostly clear this \nmorning, especially areas north to northwest of Flagstaff, near the \nGrand Canyon, and eastern portions of the CWA, including most of \nNavajo and Apache counties, are starting to see new showers and \nthunderstorms develop near higher terrain and where daytime heating \nwas the greatest today. This activity is expected to continue through\nthe rest of the afternoon and early evening hours before settling \ndown tonight.\n\nFor Wednesday onward, the high pressure that was over New Mexico\nwill strengthen and shift westward and over Arizona, leading to \nan overall decrease in showers and thunderstorms as subsidence \nincreases and drier, mid-level air moves into the region. \nHowever, this will not completely stop the low-level monsoonal \nmoisture, so isolated to low-end scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will still be possible over the higher terrain \nduring the late morning and afternoon hours. The decrease in \nMonsoon, along with the strengthening and relocation of the area \nof high pressure, will cause temperatures to rise above normal by \nThursday, with a more significant rise for Friday through Sunday. \nAn Extreme Heat Warning for the Grand Canyon (below 4000 ft) will \nstart at 10 MST Thursday, with additional warnings starting 10 \nMST/ 11 AM MDT Friday for Yavapai County and northern Gila County \n(both below 4000 ft), Marble and Glen Canyon, including the city of \nPage, and Oak Creek and Sycamore Canyons, including the city of \nSedona.",
